e_id = found id? Probably yes, set bd_issue_id to the existing issue id. Zero bd create calls.\n  - not found: bd create in target repo cwd → success: decision `filed`, bd_issue_id = new id. Failure: decision `skipped`, reason `bd_failure`.\n  - only closed holds token: search --status open finds nothing → create → filed.\n\nBut brief 2.2 says \"→ proposed + filed\". Hmm, \"proposed\" maybe means the proposal remains and is filed. Decision `filed`.\n\nWait 2.2 test description: \"only a CLOSED issue holds the token → new issue proposed and filed (closed must not block re-filing a regressed fault)\". So decision would be... maybe just filed with new id.\n\n2.6: \"bd create fails (non-beads dir) → decision skipped, reason bd_failure, no error propagated.\"\n\n2.3 (tasks): implement target-repo search `bd search --status open <token>` (list + grep fallback). Note: bd search output parsing fragile — follow-up threshold: if parsing fragile, stop and record.\n\nAlso brief: \"bd export -o .beads/issues.jsonl afterwards if the target repo has one (tracked-export invariant) — test that too.\"\n\nHmm, tricky: bd embedded with no-db... In test temp repo we do `bd init`, create issue via routing code, then routing should run `bd export -o .beads/issues.jsonl`. Test asserts the file exists/contains the issue.\n\nLet me check bd CLI behavior first: how does bd search output look, does bd create print the issue id? Let me experiment in a temp dir (allowed — never touch .beads of family repos; temp dirs fine).\n\nAlso check how dedup search finds token in body: bd search searches title/body? The brief says \"seed an OPEN issue whose body contains token\". So search must match body. bd search may only search titles... Need to test. Fallback: `bd list` + grep. Tasks 2.3 mention \"(list + grep fallback with the same status filter)\". Hmm, \"list + grep fallback\" — maybe bd list --status open and grep body. Let's experiment.\n\nAlso bd init flags: `bd init` may need --no-db or similar