{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":1061,"output":1025,"cacheRead":19712,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":835,"totalTokens":21798,"cost":{"input":0.00015915,"output":0.0005124999999999999,"cacheRead":0.00059136,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.00126301}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Design: `bd_issue_id` set only for `filed`... but what about `existing`? Design says bd_issue_id set only for filed. Hmm, but for `existing` it might be useful to know the id. Design line 64: \"`bd_issue_id` is set only for `filed`.\" Wait — but then dedup test can't assert id. Brief 2.1: \"→ decision `existing`, zero `bd create` calls\". We can assert zero bd create calls how? Can't easily observe. Could assert via... maybe wrap bd invocation so test can inject a spy? Simplest: make the filing function take a bd runner closure? Or: assert decision existing and that bd_issue_id... hmm.\n\nAlternative: for `existing`, we could capture the existing issue's id from search output and set bd_issue_id — that would violate design (\"set only for filed\"). Let me re-read: line 59-64: `\"bd_issue_id\": null` in schema example, \"`bd_issue_id` is set only for `filed`\". OK so keep null for existing. Hmm, but that loses info. Design is authoritative; but maybe setting it for existing is also reasonable... Stick to design: only for filed.\n\nHow to assert \"zero bd create calls\"? Use dependency injection: a `Bd` trait or a runner function param. Given the \"process-boundary\" isolation helper suggestion (\"If spawning bd in-process is flaky in tests, isolate via a helper that sets the subprocess cwd\"), I could design:\n\n```rust\npub trait BdRunner {\n    fn run(&self, cwd: &Path, args: &[&str]) -> Result<Output, String>;\n}\n```\n\nOr simpler: pass a closure `bd: &dyn Fn(&Path, &[&str]) -> std::io::Result<std::process::Output>`. Tests inject a spy that records calls and delegates to real bd. That makes \"zero bd create calls\" assertable and makes failing-create tests deterministic (2.6: non-beads dir → real bd create fails anyway, exit code nonzero — that works with real bd too).\n\nActually for 2.6 the brief says use a real non-beads dir — real bd create fails there. Fine without spy. But for 2.1 zero-create assertion, spy is cleanest. Let me design API:\n\n```rust\npub fn file_proposals(\n    proposals: &mut [ProposalRow],\n    file: bool,\n    run_bd: &dyn Fn(&std::path::Path, &[&str]) -> Result<std::process::Output, String>,\n) -> Result<(), String>\n```\n\nHmm, or return new Vec. Let me think about what the CLI wiring (out of scope, o3j) will need: proposals list, mutate decisions in place. I'll make it take `&mut [ProposalRow]` and return `Result<Vec<String>, String>`? Simpler: return Result<(), String> and mutate in place; loud errors only for... design says bd failure never fatal. Errors like missing bd binary → spawn err → treated as bd_failure? Design: \"`bd` binary missing\" → skipped/bd_failure. So spawn errors also map to bd_failure for create. For search, if bd search fails (non-beads dir)? Not spec'd... If repo isn't a beads repo, search fails → treat like no match → then create fails → skipped/bd_failure. That composes fine.\n\nNow bd CLI behavior — let me experiment in temp dir:\n- `bd init` (what flags? maybe `bd init --no-db`? The charly repo config has no-db: true. Let's see what `bd init` produces and what search output looks like.)\n- `bd create \"title\" --body`? check flags.\n- `bd search --status open <query>` — does it match body?\n- `bd list --status open` output format.\n\nAlso HOME isolation: bd might use a global ~/.beads or config. Let's test."}}
