{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":456,"output":2185,"cacheRead":27904,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":2089,"totalTokens":30545,"cost":{"input":0.0000684,"output":0.0010925,"cacheRead":0.0008371199999999999,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.0019980199999999997}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Confirmed: `bd search` positional/title query does NOT match description, and combined positional+desc-contains returns [] (AND semantics: title must match too). So the reliable dedup is `bd list --status open --json` + grep description — exactly the documented fallback. I'll implement: try `bd search <token> --desc-contains <token> --status open --json`... no wait, that returns [] even when there IS a match in description (since title doesn't contain token) — it's not just fragile, it's wrong for our token-in-body scheme. So primary path: use search with --desc-contains ONLY via... hmm, `bd search --query \"\"`? Empty query errored.\n\nGiven the design/task text says \"bd search --status open <token> (list + grep fallback with the same status filter)\", I'll implement: \n1. `bd search <token> --status open --json` → if error or empty →\n2. fallback: `bd list --status open --json` → parse JSON, check description/title contains token.\n\nThis satisfies \"same status filter\" for both. And since our tokens live in bodies, effectively list+grep decides. That's robust (JSON parsing, not stdout text parsing). \n\nDesign decision for create title/body: token goes in BODY (per design decision 2). Title: something human-readable like \"[evallerina] tool_fault: <scenario>\". Description/body: token + tool version. Let me create with: `bd create --title \"<scenario>: <fault-class>\" --description \"<token>\\ntool: <tool>\" --json`. Check bd create flag for title: positional? `bd create \"Test token...\" --body ...` worked with positional title and --body flag. Also `-d/--description`. Use positional title + `-d` description + `--json` → parse \"id\".\n\nNow architecture of the code:\n\n```rust\n/// Runner abstraction so tests can spy on bd invocations.\npub type BdOutput = Result<std::process::Output, String>;\npub fn run_bd(cwd: &Path, args: &[&str]) -> BdOutput { ... real impl ... }\n\npub fn file_proposals(\n    proposals: &mut [ProposalRow],\n    file_authorized: bool,\n    run_bd: &dyn Fn(&Path, &[&str]) -> BdOutput,\n) -> Result<usize, String>  // count filed? or ()\n```\n\nPer-row:\n- decision must be actionable: only rows with repo Some and decision \"dry_run\" participate? Also skipped/unmapped stay. If file_authorized == false → leave everything (dry-run), return.\n- authorized: for each row with repo Some:\n  - search: run_bd(repo_cwd, [\"search\", token, \"--status\", \"open\", \"--json\"]) → ok && stdout parse array non-empty → decision \"existing\", reason None... wait schema: reason null except skipped. So existing → reason None. bd_issue_id stays None (per design). Hmm — but then 2.1's \"zero bd create calls\" assert via spy.\n  - if search command fails (non-zero/err) → fallback list: run_bd(cwd, [\"list\",\"--status\",\"open\",\"--json\"]) → parse JSON array, any entry with description or title containing token → existing.\n  - not found → create: run_bd(cwd, [\"create\", title, \"-d\", body, \"--json\"]) → success: parse JSON id → decision \"filed\", bd_issue_id Some(id). Then export: run_bd(cwd, [\"export\",\"-o\",\".beads/issues.jsonl\"]) — failure tolerated? Brief: \"bd export afterwards if the target repo has one (tracked-export invariant) — test that too.\" If export fails... I'd say record but not fatal? Keep simple: ignore export failure? Hmm, invariant says must export. But bd_failure reason is for create. I'll run export and ignore its failure (or treat failure as non-fatal). Actually to be safe: run export; if it fails, we don't fail the report (design: filing is optimization). Keep silent-ignore but maybe include in... no field for it. Ignore.\n  - create failure (spawn error or non-zero exit): decision \"skipped\", reason \"bd_failure\", bd_issue_id None.\n\nDecision values on rows already \"skipped\"/unmapped_tool: untouched.\n\nWhat does file_proposals return? Maybe Vec of counts or just (). CLI wiring is out of scope; keep it simple: `pub fn file_proposals(proposals: &mut [ProposalRow], run_bd: &dyn Fn(...)) -> Result<(), String>` — file_authorized flag needed? The CLI decides; if not authorized it just doesn't call. But tasks/design emphasize dry-run default. I'll include `file_authorized: bool` param for explicitness — if false, no-op. Hmm, that's redundant but matches \"--file-authorized path\". Actually simpler API without the flag; caller wires --file. But design decision 3: \"--route --file additionally shells out to bd\". The routing module function taking a bool makes the default explicit and testable. I'll keep the bool.\n\nAlso spawn env isolation: run_bd uses Command::new(\"bd\").current_dir(cwd). Env: bd might pick up BEADS_DIR etc. Fine.\n\nSpy for tests: closure capturing RefCell<Vec<(PathBuf, Vec<String>)>> delegating to real run_bd. \n\nNow tests (RED first). Per brief sequence, 4 red→green cycles with atomic commits:\n\nCommit 1 (RED 2.1 → GREEN): test dedup_open_issue_blocks_filing:\n- temp dir fixture: mktemp under std::env::temp_dir(), git init + bd init (via std::process::Command in test setup — is that \"touching .beads of family repos\"? No — temp dir only. Allowed per brief).\n- seed: bd create with token in body (open).\n- build proposal row for that repo (construct ProposalRow directly rather than via propose? tests can construct ProposalRow {tool:\"wai\", repo:Some(temp_repo_path), scenario, fault_class:\"tool_fault\", token, decision:\"dry_run\", ...}).\n- spy runner records create calls.\n- file_proposals(&mut [row], true, spy) → row.decision == \"existing\", spy asserts no \"create\" args.\n\nCommit 2 (RED 2.2 → GREEN): only closed issue holds token → filed:\n- seed issue, close it.\n- file → decision \"filed\", bd_issue_id Some(new id) != old id. Also closed didn't block.\n\nWait brief says \"→ proposed + filed\". Test asserts decision filed + id captured.\n\nCommit 3 (RED 2.3/2.4/2.5 → GREEN): bd create invoked in target repo cwd, captures exit code + id; export test:\n- test filing_creates_issue_in_target_repo_cwd: temp repo bd init, no seed. file → decision filed, bd_issue_id starts with repo prefix; verify issue exists via bd list in that repo (title match). Verify cwd: spy records cwd — assert cwd == repo path. Also assert create args include token in description.\n- test export: after filing, .beads/issues.jsonl exists in temp repo and contains the new id. This asserts our code ran bd export. \n\nCommit 4 (RED 2.6 → GREEN): create fails in non-beads temp dir (no bd init) → decision skipped, reason bd_failure, file_proposals returns Ok, other rows still processed.\n\nBrief says gate: cargo test --test routing green after each step. So each commit: red test + impl together? \"RED X: ... GREEN.\" means write failing test, then implement, then commit atomically per green step. Since test+impl in same commit, fine.\n\nTDD discipline: write test, run (red), implement, run (green), commit.\n\nFixture helper in tests:\n\n```rust\nstruct TempRepo { path: PathBuf } // cleanup on drop (remove_dir_all)\nfn temp_repo() -> TempRepo {\n    let dir = std::env::temp_dir().join(format!(\"evallerina-routing-test-{}-{}\", std::process::id(), uuid-ish));\n    fs::create_dir_all(&dir);\n    run git init, bd init\n}\n```\n\nUnique suffix: use SystemTime nanos + counter.\n\nNote git hooks: bd init printed \"Preserving existing pre-commit hook from ~/.git-hooks\" — because git init inherits template? It said preserving existing hooks — fine, temp repo, harmless. Actually we don't even need git init for bd to work? bd init in plain dir — earlier `bd create` failed in non-beads dir; bd init probably works without git. Let me not git init — simpler, and \"non-beads dir\" fixture is just a plain dir. Quick check: bd init in non-git dir works? Let me verify when writing tests. Also isolation: bd might touch global ~/.beads? bd init output mentioned \"Repository ID... Clone ID\" — embedded dolt in .beads dir of cwd presumably. To be safe set env BD_...? Keep it; cwd-scoped.\n\nOne concern: does bd init/create write anywhere outside cwd (like ~/.beads global)? Test: run in temp, check for stray. I'll trust cwd scoping (bd discovered \".beads\" relative). The \"beads_global\" only with --global flag.\n\nAlso note: `bd search --json` output when no matches — prints \"[]\"? Earlier text search printed \"No issues found matching...\" with exit=0. With --json probably []. Parsing handles both.\n\nNow write the code. First RED 2.1. Let me add test + minimal stub? TDD: write failing test, run cargo test (fails to compile since file_proposals doesn't exist — that counts as red), then implement.\n\nTest construction of ProposalRow: fields are pub. Good.\n\nLet me now write RED 2.1 test."}}
